ADDART({id:"protoceratops",t:"Protoceratops",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Protoceratops",r:["triceratops","velociraptor","oviraptor","dinoeggs","fossils","paleontology","styracosaurus"],
p:[["A small horned dinosaur","An early relative of Triceratops, about the size of a sheep."],["The fighting dinosaurs","One was found locked in combat with a Velociraptor."],["Gobi Desert","Hundreds of skeletons have been found in Mongolia and China."]],
l:"Protoceratops was a plant-eating dinosaur that lived in the deserts of what are now Mongolia and northern China about 75 to 71 million years ago, in the Late Cretaceous. About 1.8 meters long and weighing around 80 kilograms, it was roughly the size of a sheep. It belonged to the ceratopsians, the horned dinosaurs, but unlike its giant later relative Triceratops it had no real horns: instead it had a parrot-like beak, a bony frill at the back of its skull and a small bump on its nose. Protoceratops is one of the best-known dinosaurs because hundreds of skeletons have been found, from tiny hatchlings to old adults, letting scientists study how it grew. Its most famous fossil, found in 1971, is the ‘Fighting Dinosaurs’: a Protoceratops and a Velociraptor preserved in the middle of a struggle, the Velociraptor’s claw in the Protoceratops’s neck and its arm caught in the Protoceratops’s beak, both apparently buried by a collapsing sand dune. Some historians have suggested that Protoceratops skulls, found by ancient travelers in Central Asia, may have inspired legends of the griffin.",
s:[["Discovery","The first Protoceratops fossils were found in 1922 at the Flaming Cliffs in Mongolia by the Central Asiatic Expeditions of the American Museum of Natural History. It was named in 1923. Eggs found nearby were at first thought to be its own, but most were later shown to belong to Oviraptor relatives."],["The Fighting Dinosaurs","The fossil was found by a Polish–Mongolian expedition in 1971 and is a national treasure of Mongolia. It is one of very few fossils that show two dinosaurs interacting at the moment of death."],["Frills","The size and shape of the frill varied between individuals and grew larger with age, suggesting it was used for display, perhaps to attract mates or show maturity."],["Nests and young","A 2011 study described a nest with 15 juvenile Protoceratops, suggesting that young stayed together, and possibly that parents cared for them for a time."]],
k:[["Lived","About 75–71 million years ago"],["Where","Mongolia and northern China"],["Length","About 1.8 meters"],["Weight","About 80 kilograms"],["Group","Ceratopsian (horned dinosaurs)"],["Famous fossil","The Fighting Dinosaurs (1971)"]],
d:[["1922","First fossils found at the Flaming Cliffs"],["1923","Protoceratops named"],["1971","Fighting Dinosaurs fossil found"],["2011","Nest of 15 juveniles described"]],
s2:[["Griffin legend","The folklorist Adrienne Mayor suggested that ancient gold miners who found beaked Protoceratops skulls in the Gobi may have imagined the griffin, a beaked, four-legged monster."],["Many fossils","So many Protoceratops have been found that it is sometimes called the ‘sheep of the Cretaceous’."]],
g:[["Ceratopsian","A horned, frilled, beaked dinosaur"],["Frill","A bony shield at the back of the skull"],["Hatchling","A newly hatched animal"],["Gobi Desert","A large desert in Mongolia and China"]],
f:["Protoceratops was a relative of Triceratops.","A Protoceratops was found fighting a Velociraptor.","Protoceratops lived in Mongolia."],
fun:"The ‘Fighting Dinosaurs’ fossil shows a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops frozen in battle for 70 million years."});

ADDART({id:"giganotosaurus",t:"Giganotosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Giganotosaurus",r:["trex","carnotaurus","argentinosaurus","spinosaurus","fossils","allosaurus"],
p:[["A rival to T. rex","One of the largest meat-eating dinosaurs."],["South American giant","It lived in Argentina about 98 million years ago."],["Found by a car mechanic","An amateur fossil hunter discovered it in 1993."]],
l:"Giganotosaurus was one of the largest meat-eating dinosaurs ever to walk on land. It lived in what is now Patagonia, Argentina, about 99 to 97 million years ago, in the Late Cretaceous, about 30 million years before Tyrannosaurus rex. It is estimated to have been about 12 to 13 meters long and to have weighed around 6 to 8 tonnes, similar to or slightly larger than T. rex in length, though probably less heavily built. Its name means ‘giant southern lizard’. Giganotosaurus had a long, narrow skull up to about 1.6 meters long, filled with thin, blade-like teeth for slicing flesh, rather than the thick, bone-crushing teeth of T. rex. It belonged to the carcharodontosaurids, a family of huge predators related to Allosaurus. It lived alongside some of the biggest sauropods, such as Andesaurus and possibly relatives of Argentinosaurus, and some scientists think it and its relatives may have hunted these giants, perhaps in groups. The first skeleton was discovered in 1993 by Rubén Carolini, an amateur fossil hunter and auto mechanic, near the town of Villa El Chocón.",
s:[["Discovery","Rubén Carolini found the bones while driving a dune buggy in the badlands of Neuquén Province in July 1993. The dinosaur was named Giganotosaurus carolinii in his honor in 1995 by Rodolfo Coria and Leonardo Salgado. The skeleton is about 70 percent complete."],["T. rex or Giganotosaurus?","Which was bigger depends on which specimens are compared. A second, partial jaw suggested a larger individual. Many scientists now think the two were similar in length, with T. rex heavier and having a stronger bite."],["Slicing teeth","Giganotosaurus’s teeth were flat and serrated like steak knives. It may have attacked large prey with repeated slashing bites, causing blood loss, rather than crushing bones."],["Fast for its size?","A 2020s study suggested Giganotosaurus could reach speeds of about 50 kilometers per hour, though estimates for such large animals are uncertain."]],
k:[["Lived","About 99–97 million years ago"],["Where","Patagonia, Argentina"],["Length","About 12–13 meters"],["Weight","About 6–8 tonnes"],["Teeth","Blade-like, for slicing"],["Name means","Giant southern lizard"]],
d:[["1993","Skeleton found by Rubén Carolini"],["1995","Giganotosaurus named"],["2022","Appears in the film Jurassic World Dominion"]],
s2:[["Museum","The original skeleton is displayed at the Ernesto Bachmann Paleontological Museum in Villa El Chocón, Argentina."],["Relatives in Africa","Its close relative Carcharodontosaurus, ‘shark-toothed lizard’, lived in North Africa at about the same time."]],
g:[["Carcharodontosaurid","A family of giant meat-eating dinosaurs"],["Serrated","Having a saw-like edge"],["Patagonia","The southern region of Argentina and Chile"],["Badlands","Dry land with eroded rock"]],
f:["Giganotosaurus lived before T. rex.","Giganotosaurus had blade-like teeth.","A car mechanic discovered Giganotosaurus."],
fun:"Giganotosaurus and T. rex never met: they lived about 30 million years and thousands of kilometers apart."});

ADDART({id:"edmontosaurus",t:"Edmontosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦆",w:"Edmontosaurus",r:["parasaurolophus","maiasaura","trex","dino","fossils","corythosaurus"],
p:[["A duck-billed giant","A large plant-eater with a flat, beak-like snout."],["Dinosaur mummies","Some fossils preserve skin and a fleshy crest."],["T. rex’s prey","One has a healed T. rex bite on its tail."]],
l:"Edmontosaurus was a large plant-eating duck-billed dinosaur, or hadrosaur, that lived in western North America about 73 to 66 million years ago, right up to the asteroid impact that ended the age of dinosaurs. It grew to about 9 to 12 meters long and weighed around 4 to 6 tonnes or more. It had a long, flat, toothless beak at the front of its snout for cropping plants, and hundreds of tightly packed teeth further back, arranged in rows called dental batteries, which ground tough plants such as conifer needles and twigs. Edmontosaurus could walk on two legs or four. It is one of the best-known dinosaurs: many skeletons have been found, including bone beds with thousands of animals, suggesting they lived in large herds. Several remarkable ‘mummies’ preserve impressions of scaly skin, and one, found in 2013, shows a soft, fleshy crest on its head like a rooster’s comb. Edmontosaurus lived alongside Tyrannosaurus rex and Triceratops, and one skeleton has a T. rex tooth wound on its tail that had healed, proving it escaped an attack.",
s:[["Dental batteries","Edmontosaurus had up to about 60 columns of teeth in each jaw, with new teeth constantly replacing worn ones. Studies of tooth wear show it ground food with a sideways chewing motion, much like a cow."],["Mummies","In 1908 the fossil hunter Charles Sternberg and his sons found an Edmontosaurus ‘mummy’ in Wyoming with skin impressions over much of its body. Another specimen, nicknamed ‘Dakota’ and found in North Dakota in 1999, preserves skin and soft tissue in three dimensions."],["Escaping T. rex","A skeleton at the Denver Museum of Nature and Science has a bite mark on its tail matching a T. rex, with bone that had healed afterward. It is evidence that T. rex hunted living prey, not just carcasses."],["Arctic dinosaurs","Edmontosaurus fossils have been found in Alaska, north of the Arctic Circle, where they would have faced months of winter darkness."]],
k:[["Lived","About 73–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 9–12 meters"],["Diet","Plants"],["Group","Hadrosaur (duck-billed dinosaur)"],["Teeth","Hundreds, in dental batteries"]],
d:[["1908","Sternberg ‘mummy’ found in Wyoming"],["1917","Edmontosaurus named"],["1999","‘Dakota’ mummy found"],["2013","Fleshy head crest described"]],
s2:[["Named after a city","Edmontosaurus was named after Edmonton, Canada, because its fossils were first found in rocks called the Edmonton Formation."],["Herds","Bone beds in Alaska, Wyoming and South Dakota contain huge numbers of Edmontosaurus, perhaps killed by floods or droughts."]],
g:[["Hadrosaur","A duck-billed plant-eating dinosaur"],["Dental battery","Rows of tightly packed replacement teeth"],["Mummy","A fossil preserving skin or soft tissue"],["Herd","A group of animals living together"]],
f:["Edmontosaurus was a duck-billed dinosaur.","Some Edmontosaurus fossils preserve skin.","One survived a T. rex bite."],
fun:"Edmontosaurus could have hundreds of teeth in its mouth at once."});

ADDART({id:"sarcosuchus",t:"Sarcosuchus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐊",w:"Sarcosuchus",r:["croc","deinosuchus","spinosaurus","fossils","sahara","paleontology"],
p:[["SuperCroc","One of the largest crocodile relatives ever."],["Sahara swamps","It lived where the Sahara Desert is now."],["A bulb on its snout","Its snout ended in a strange swelling."]],
l:"Sarcosuchus was a gigantic relative of crocodiles that lived about 112 million years ago, in the Early Cretaceous, in the rivers of what are now Niger and other parts of North Africa, and in Brazil, which was then joined to Africa. Nicknamed ‘SuperCroc’, it was about 9 to 9.5 meters long and weighed around 3 to 4 tonnes, roughly twice the length of the largest living crocodiles. Its name means ‘flesh crocodile’. Sarcosuchus had a long, narrow snout, about 1.6 meters long in the largest skulls, with over 100 teeth, and at the tip a large bony bulb, called a bulla, whose purpose is unknown. Its eyes pointed upward, so it could lie hidden in the water watching for prey. It probably ate fish, turtles and other animals, perhaps including dinosaurs that came to the water’s edge. Although named in 1966, it became famous in 2000 when the American paleontologist Paul Sereno and his team found several skulls in the Ténéré Desert of Niger, in rocks from a time when the Sahara was a land of rivers and swamps.",
s:[["SuperCroc","Sereno’s expedition found the most complete skull and parts of the skeleton in 1997 and 2000. Growth rings in its armored back plates suggest it took about 50 to 60 years to reach full size, growing throughout its life."],["The mysterious bulb","Similar swellings on the snouts of male gharials, a living crocodilian, are used to make sounds and attract mates. Sarcosuchus’s bulla may have had a similar purpose, but scientists cannot be sure."],["A green Sahara","About 110 million years ago, the area of Niger had broad rivers and floodplains. Sarcosuchus shared it with dinosaurs such as Suchomimus and Ouranosaurus."],["Not a direct ancestor","Sarcosuchus was not a true crocodile but belonged to an extinct group of crocodile relatives called pholidosaurids."]],
k:[["Lived","About 112 million years ago"],["Where","North Africa and Brazil"],["Length","About 9–9.5 meters"],["Weight","About 3–4 tonnes"],["Nickname","SuperCroc"],["Name means","Flesh crocodile"]],
d:[["1966","Sarcosuchus named from fossils in Niger"],["1997","Sereno’s team finds a large skull"],["2000","More fossils found; ‘SuperCroc’ becomes famous"],["2001","SuperCroc study published"]],
s2:[["Big appetite","Like modern crocodiles, Sarcosuchus may have gone weeks between large meals."],["Armor","Its back was covered with bony plates called osteoderms, each up to about 25 centimeters long."]],
g:[["Crocodilian","Crocodiles, alligators and their relatives"],["Bulla","A rounded bony swelling"],["Osteoderm","A bony plate in the skin"],["Floodplain","Flat land beside a river that floods"]],
f:["Sarcosuchus is nicknamed SuperCroc.","Sarcosuchus lived where the Sahara is now.","It had a bulb at the tip of its snout."],
fun:"A Sarcosuchus skull was about as long as an adult person is tall."});

ADDART({id:"megalania",t:"Megalania",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Megalania",r:["australiacountry","komodo","iceagemammals","thylacine","fossils","humanorigins","diprotodon"],
p:[["A giant lizard","The largest land lizard known."],["Ice Age Australia","It lived until about 50,000 years ago."],["Komodo’s relative","A huge cousin of the Komodo dragon."]],
l:"Megalania was a giant monitor lizard that lived in Australia during the Pleistocene, the time of the ice ages, until about 50,000 years ago. It is the largest land lizard known to have lived. Its size is debated because no complete skeleton has been found, but recent estimates suggest it was about 3.5 to 7 meters long and weighed perhaps 300 to 600 kilograms, much larger than its living relative, the Komodo dragon, which reaches about 3 meters. Megalania had a large head with curved, serrated teeth and powerful limbs, and was the top land predator of its time in Australia, probably hunting large animals such as giant kangaroos and the huge wombat relative Diprotodon. Like the Komodo dragon, it may have had venom glands. Scientists now often place it in the same genus as the Komodo dragon, calling it Varanus priscus. Megalania disappeared around the time that people first arrived in Australia, when many of the continent’s giant animals, called megafauna, became extinct. Whether humans, climate change or both caused these extinctions is still debated.",
s:[["Discovery","The British scientist Richard Owen named Megalania in 1859 from fossil bones sent from Australia. The name means ‘great roamer’."],["How big?","Early estimates of 7 to 9 meters were based on large individual bones. More careful studies give smaller sizes, but even the lower estimates make it far bigger than any living lizard."],["Venom?","Komodo dragons and some other monitor lizards have venom glands in their jaws. Because Megalania was closely related, scientists think it may also have been venomous, which would have made it one of the largest venomous animals ever."],["Megafauna","Australia’s Ice Age megafauna included Diprotodon, the size of a rhinoceros; Procoptodon, a giant short-faced kangaroo; Thylacoleo, the marsupial lion; and Genyornis, a giant flightless bird."]],
k:[["Lived","Until about 50,000 years ago"],["Where","Australia"],["Length","About 3.5–7 meters (debated)"],["Weight","About 300–600 kilograms"],["Relatives","Komodo dragon and other monitors"],["Scientific name","Varanus priscus"]],
d:[["1859","Richard Owen names Megalania"],["2009","Study suggests it may have been venomous"],["2014","Fossils from Timor suggest relatives lived beyond Australia"]],
s2:[["Living giants","The Komodo dragon, found on a few islands of Indonesia, is the largest living lizard and gives an idea of what Megalania was like."],["Meeting people","The first people reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago, and some may have encountered Megalania."]],
g:[["Monitor lizard","A large lizard with a long neck and forked tongue"],["Pleistocene","The epoch of the ice ages"],["Megafauna","Very large animals"],["Venom","A poison injected by a bite or sting"]],
f:["Megalania was a giant lizard.","Megalania lived in Australia.","It was related to the Komodo dragon."],
fun:"Megalania may have been the largest venomous animal ever to walk on land."});

ADDART({id:"nothosaurus",t:"Nothosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦭",w:"Nothosaurus",r:["marinereptiles","elasmosaurus","ichthyosaurs","pangaea","fossils"],
p:[["Early sea reptile","It hunted fish in shallow Triassic seas."],["Half land, half sea","It may have rested on shores like a seal."],["Plesiosaur relative","An ancestor-like cousin of plesiosaurs."]],
l:"Nothosaurus was a sea reptile that lived about 240 to 210 million years ago, in the Triassic Period, in the shallow seas that covered parts of what are now Europe, North Africa, the Middle East and China, along the edges of the supercontinent Pangaea. It had a long neck and tail, a flattened skull with many long, sharp, interlocking teeth for catching fish, and paddle-like limbs with webbed toes. Most species were 1 to 4 meters long, though the largest, Nothosaurus giganteus, may have reached around 7 meters. Nothosaurus could swim well, probably using its legs and tail, but its limbs could still support it on land, and it may have come ashore to rest and lay eggs, perhaps like seals or sea lions today. It belonged to a group called the nothosaurs, close relatives of the plesiosaurs, the long-necked sea reptiles of the Jurassic and Cretaceous. Its name means ‘false lizard’. Nothosaurus fossils are common in Germany, where the first were found in the 1830s, and beautiful specimens have also been found in southern China.",
s:[["Catching fish","Nothosaurus’s long jaws snapped sideways to catch fish, and its teeth meshed together to trap slippery prey. Its eyes faced upward, perhaps to spot fish above it."],["Life near the shore","Nothosaurs lived in warm, shallow seas and lagoons. Fossil trackways and bone structure suggest they were mainly water animals that could move on land."],["Relatives","The nothosaurs and their relatives, such as Pachypleurosaurus and Placodus, together called sauropterygians, gave rise to the plesiosaurs, which became fully adapted to life in the open ocean."],["Fossils in China","Since the 2000s, many well-preserved Nothosaurus and other Triassic marine reptiles have been found in Guizhou and Yunnan provinces of China."]],
k:[["Lived","About 240–210 million years ago (Triassic)"],["Where","Shallow seas of Europe, Africa, Middle East, China"],["Length","Mostly 1–4 meters; largest about 7 m"],["Diet","Fish"],["Relatives","Plesiosaurs"],["Name means","False lizard"]],
d:[["1834","Nothosaurus named in Germany"],["2010s","Many new specimens described from China"]],
s2:[["Not a dinosaur","Like other sea reptiles of the time, Nothosaurus was not a dinosaur, though it lived in the same era as early dinosaurs."],["Monte San Giorgio","The Monte San Giorgio fossil site on the Swiss–Italian border, a World Heritage Site, preserves many Triassic sea reptiles."]],
g:[["Triassic","The period from about 252 to 201 million years ago"],["Sauropterygian","A group of sea reptiles including plesiosaurs"],["Paddle","A flat limb used for swimming"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water near the sea"]],
f:["Nothosaurus was a sea reptile.","Nothosaurus lived in the Triassic.","Nothosaurus was related to plesiosaurs."],
fun:"Nothosaurus may have hauled itself onto beaches to rest, like a seal."});

ADDART({id:"logarithms",t:"Logarithms",c:"Mathematics",i:"📈",w:"Logarithm",r:["percent","prime","pi","quake","acid","binary"],
p:[["The opposite of powers","A logarithm asks: what power gives this number?"],["Taming huge numbers","They turn multiplication into addition."],["Everyday scales","Earthquakes, sound and acidity use logarithmic scales."]],
l:"A logarithm answers the question: to what power must a base number be raised to get another number? For example, 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000, so 10 raised to the power 3 is 1,000, and the logarithm of 1,000 to base 10 is 3. Logarithms were invented by the Scottish mathematician John Napier, who published them in 1614, to make calculations easier. Their key property is that they turn multiplication into addition: the logarithm of a product equals the sum of the logarithms. Before calculators, scientists, navigators and engineers used books of logarithm tables and a tool called the slide rule, which has logarithmic scales, to multiply and divide large numbers quickly. Logarithms are also used to describe things that vary over enormous ranges. On a logarithmic scale, each step represents a multiplication rather than an addition: an earthquake of magnitude 6 releases about 32 times more energy than one of magnitude 5, sound levels in decibels rise by 10 for every tenfold increase in intensity, and pH measures acidity on a logarithmic scale.",
s:[["How they work","Common logarithms use base 10, so log 100 = 2 and log 0.01 = −2. Natural logarithms use the special number e, about 2.718, which appears in growth, interest and nature. Computer scientists often use base 2, because computers work with binary."],["Napier and Briggs","Napier spent about 20 years computing his tables. The English mathematician Henry Briggs then developed base-10 logarithms, publishing tables in 1617 and 1624, which became standard for 350 years."],["The slide rule","Invented by William Oughtred around 1622, the slide rule lets users multiply by sliding two logarithmic scales alongside each other. Engineers used slide rules to design bridges, airplanes and the Apollo spacecraft before electronic calculators appeared in the 1970s."],["Logarithmic scales","The Richter and moment magnitude scales for earthquakes, the decibel scale for sound, the pH scale for acidity and the magnitude scale for star brightness are all logarithmic. Graphs with logarithmic axes help show data that grows exponentially."]],
k:[["Question it answers","What power gives this number?"],["Invented by","John Napier (1614)"],["Key property","Turns multiplication into addition"],["Common bases","10, e (about 2.718) and 2"],["Example","log₁₀ 1,000 = 3"],["Used in","Earthquake, sound and pH scales"]],
d:[["1614","Napier publishes logarithms"],["1617","Briggs publishes base-10 tables"],["c. 1622","Oughtred invents the slide rule"],["1972","Pocket scientific calculators begin replacing slide rules"]],
s2:[["Benford’s law","In many real-world sets of numbers, such as populations and prices, the first digit is more often 1 than 9, a pattern explained using logarithms and used to detect fraud."],["Our senses","Human senses respond roughly logarithmically: doubling the loudness of a sound does not sound twice as loud."]],
g:[["Logarithm","The power a base must be raised to"],["Base","The number that is raised to a power"],["Exponent","A power, such as the 3 in 10³"],["Logarithmic scale","A scale where each step multiplies"]],
f:["Logarithms were invented by John Napier.","Logarithms turn multiplication into addition.","The decibel scale is logarithmic."],
fun:"Engineers used slide rules, based on logarithms, to help design the spacecraft that went to the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"buoyancy",t:"Density and Buoyancy",c:"Science",i:"🛟",w:"Buoyancy",r:["fluids","ships","icebergs","atom","submarine","surfacetension"],
p:[["Why things float","An upward push from water or air."],["Density matters","Objects less dense than water float."],["Archimedes’ discovery","A Greek thinker explained it over 2,000 years ago."]],
l:"Density is how much mass is packed into a given volume. A block of steel is much denser than a block of wood the same size, because its particles are heavier and packed more tightly. Water has a density of about 1 gram per cubic centimeter. Buoyancy is the upward force that a liquid or gas pushes on an object placed in it. The ancient Greek scientist Archimedes discovered that this upward force equals the weight of the fluid the object pushes aside, or displaces; this is called Archimedes’ principle. If an object weighs less than the water it displaces, it floats; if it weighs more, it sinks. That is why a huge steel ship floats: its hollow hull, full of air, displaces a large volume of water whose weight equals the ship’s, so its average density is less than water’s. Ice floats because water expands when it freezes, making ice about 9 percent less dense than liquid water. Submarines sink and rise by filling tanks with water or air, fish use swim bladders, and hot-air balloons float because hot air is less dense than the cooler air around them.",
s:[["Archimedes’ ‘Eureka!’","According to legend, Archimedes was asked to check whether a king’s crown was pure gold. Stepping into a bath, he noticed the water rise and realized he could measure the crown’s volume by the water it displaced, and so find its density. He supposedly ran through the streets shouting ‘Eureka!’, meaning ‘I have found it!’"],["Salty water","Salt water is denser than fresh water, so things float more easily in it. The Dead Sea, about ten times saltier than the ocean, is so dense that people float effortlessly on its surface."],["Layers of liquids","Liquids of different densities can form layers, such as oil floating on water. Honey, water and oil poured into a glass separate into stripes, a popular experiment."],["Ice and life","Because ice floats, lakes freeze from the top down. The ice layer insulates the water beneath, allowing fish and other creatures to survive the winter."]],
k:[["Density","Mass divided by volume"],["Water’s density","About 1 g/cm³"],["Archimedes’ principle","Upward force = weight of displaced fluid"],["Floats if","Less dense than the fluid"],["Ice","About 9% less dense than liquid water"],["Densest natural element","Osmium"]],
d:[["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes describes buoyancy"],["1620s","Cornelis Drebbel builds an early submarine"],["1783","First crewed hot-air balloon flight in Paris"]],
s2:[["Osmium and lithium","Osmium, the densest element, is about 22 times denser than water. Lithium, the lightest metal, is so light it floats on water, though it reacts with it."],["Life jackets","Life jackets are filled with foam or air, which lowers a person’s average density so they float with their head above water."]],
g:[["Density","How much mass is in a given volume"],["Buoyancy","The upward push of a fluid"],["Displace","To push aside"],["Fluid","A liquid or gas"]],
f:["Archimedes explained buoyancy.","Ice is less dense than water.","Steel ships float because of their shape."],
fun:"The Dead Sea is so salty that you can lie back and read a book while floating."});

ADDART({id:"graphtheory",t:"Graph Theory and Networks",c:"Mathematics",i:"🕸️",w:"Graph theory",r:["topology","internet","maps","algorithm","socialmedia"],
p:[["Dots and lines","A graph is points joined by lines."],["The Königsberg bridges","Euler founded the subject in 1736."],["Networks everywhere","Maps, the internet and friendships are graphs."]],
l:"In mathematics, a graph is not a chart but a collection of points, called vertices or nodes, connected by lines, called edges. Graph theory studies how such networks are connected and how to move through them. It began in 1736 when the Swiss mathematician Leonhard Euler solved the puzzle of the Seven Bridges of Königsberg: could a person walk through the city, crossing each of its seven bridges exactly once? Euler showed it was impossible by reducing the city to dots for land areas and lines for bridges, and noticing that such a walk requires all but at most two dots to have an even number of lines. Today graph theory is used everywhere. Road maps and navigation apps treat intersections as nodes and roads as edges to find the shortest route. The internet, power grids, airline routes and social networks are all graphs. Biologists use graphs to study food webs and how proteins interact. Famous problems include the four color theorem, which states that any map can be colored with just four colors so that neighboring regions differ, and the traveling salesperson problem.",
s:[["The Königsberg bridges","Königsberg, now Kaliningrad in Russia, had seven bridges linking two islands and the riverbanks. Euler’s proof that no walk crossed each bridge exactly once is considered the first theorem of graph theory and an early step toward topology."],["Shortest paths","In 1956 the Dutch computer scientist Edsger Dijkstra invented a method, now called Dijkstra’s algorithm, for finding the shortest path between nodes. Variations are used in GPS navigation and internet routing."],["The four color theorem","Proposed in 1852, the four color theorem was finally proved in 1976 by Kenneth Appel and Wolfgang Haken with the help of a computer, the first major proof to rely on one."],["Small worlds","Studies of social networks suggest most people are linked by short chains of acquaintances, an idea popularized as ‘six degrees of separation’. Networks with many local links plus a few long ones are called small-world networks."]],
k:[["Graph","Points (nodes) joined by lines (edges)"],["Founded","1736, by Leonhard Euler"],["Famous puzzle","Seven Bridges of Königsberg"],["Shortest path method","Dijkstra’s algorithm (1956)"],["Four color theorem proved","1976"],["Uses","Maps, internet, social networks, biology"]],
d:[["1736","Euler solves the Königsberg bridges problem"],["1852","Four color problem proposed"],["1956","Dijkstra’s shortest path algorithm"],["1976","Four color theorem proved with a computer"],["1998","Small-world networks described by Watts and Strogatz"]],
s2:[["Search engines","Google’s original PageRank method treated web pages as nodes and links as edges, ranking pages by how many important pages linked to them."],["Kevin Bacon game","In the game ‘Six Degrees of Kevin Bacon’, players link actors through films they appeared in together, a playful example of graph theory."]],
g:[["Graph","A set of nodes connected by edges"],["Node","A point in a graph"],["Edge","A line connecting two nodes"],["Network","A system of connected things"]],
f:["Euler founded graph theory.","A graph is made of nodes and edges.","Any map can be colored with four colors."],
fun:"Euler proved the Königsberg bridges walk was impossible without ever visiting the city."});

ADDART({id:"dopplereffect",t:"The Doppler Effect",c:"Science",i:"🚑",w:"Doppler effect",r:["sound","light","expandinguniverse","radar","weatherforecast"],
p:[["Changing pitch","A siren sounds higher coming toward you and lower going away."],["Waves squeezed and stretched","Motion changes the wavelength you receive."],["From weather to galaxies","Used in radar, medicine and astronomy."]],
l:"The Doppler effect is the change in the frequency, or pitch, of a wave when the source of the wave and the observer are moving relative to each other. You hear it when an ambulance with its siren on passes by: as it approaches, the sound waves bunch up in front of it, so you hear a higher pitch; as it moves away, the waves are stretched out behind it, so the pitch drops. The effect was described in 1842 by the Austrian physicist Christian Doppler. It applies to all kinds of waves, including light: light from an object moving toward us is shifted toward shorter, bluer wavelengths, called blueshift, while light from an object moving away is shifted toward longer, redder wavelengths, called redshift. Astronomers use redshift to measure how fast stars and galaxies are moving, which led to the discovery that the universe is expanding, and to detect planets around other stars. Doppler radar measures the speed of raindrops in storms and of cars for speed cameras, and Doppler ultrasound lets doctors see blood flowing through the heart and blood vessels.",
s:[["Testing the idea","In 1845 the Dutch scientist C. H. D. Buys Ballot tested Doppler’s idea by having trumpeters play a steady note on a moving train while musicians with perfect pitch listened beside the track. They heard the pitch rise and fall as predicted."],["Redshift and the universe","In the 1920s, Edwin Hubble and others found that light from distant galaxies is redshifted, and that the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it is moving away, evidence that the universe is expanding."],["Finding planets","As a planet orbits a star, it tugs the star slightly back and forth. The tiny Doppler shifts in the star’s light revealed the first planet around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, in 1995."],["Weather radar","Doppler weather radar sends out radio pulses and measures shifts in their echoes from raindrops, showing wind speed and direction inside storms and helping forecasters spot the rotation that can form tornadoes."]],
k:[["Described by","Christian Doppler (1842)"],["Approaching source","Higher frequency"],["Receding source","Lower frequency"],["Light toward us","Blueshift"],["Light away from us","Redshift"],["Uses","Radar, ultrasound, astronomy"]],
d:[["1842","Doppler describes the effect"],["1845","Buys Ballot tests it with trumpeters on a train"],["1929","Hubble links redshift and distance"],["1995","First exoplanet around a Sun-like star found using Doppler shifts"]],
s2:[["Sonic booms","When an aircraft flies faster than sound, the sound waves pile up into a shock wave heard on the ground as a sonic boom."],["Bats","Some bats use the Doppler effect in their echolocation to detect the movement of flying insects."]],
g:[["Frequency","How many waves pass each second"],["Pitch","How high or low a sound is"],["Redshift","Light stretched to longer wavelengths"],["Blueshift","Light squeezed to shorter wavelengths"]],
f:["The Doppler effect changes the pitch of moving sounds.","Christian Doppler described the effect.","Redshift shows the universe is expanding."],
fun:"The Doppler effect was first tested with trumpeters playing on a moving train."});

ADDART({id:"momentum",t:"Momentum and Collisions",c:"Science",i:"🎱",w:"Momentum",r:["newton","friction","energyphys","rocket","grav","cars"],
p:[["Mass in motion","Momentum is mass multiplied by velocity."],["Conserved","In a collision, total momentum stays the same."],["Rockets and seatbelts","Momentum explains how both work."]],
l:"Momentum is a measure of how much motion an object has, equal to its mass multiplied by its velocity. A heavy truck moving slowly can have as much momentum as a light car moving fast. Momentum has a direction as well as a size. One of the most important laws in physics is the conservation of momentum: in a closed system, where no outside forces act, the total momentum before an event equals the total momentum after it. When two billiard balls collide, momentum passes from one to the other; when a cannon fires, the cannonball shoots forward and the cannon recoils backward with equal and opposite momentum. Rockets work the same way: they push hot gas out of their engines at high speed, and the rocket gains momentum in the opposite direction, even in the vacuum of space. Changing an object’s momentum requires a force acting over time, called impulse. Spreading a change of momentum over a longer time reduces the force, which is why airbags, seatbelts, crumple zones in cars, padded helmets and bending your knees when you land all help prevent injuries.",
s:[["Newton’s laws","Isaac Newton’s second law can be stated as: force equals the rate of change of momentum. His third law, that every action has an equal and opposite reaction, explains why momentum is conserved when objects push on each other."],["Elastic and inelastic","In an elastic collision, such as between billiard balls, both momentum and kinetic energy are kept. In an inelastic collision, such as a car crash, momentum is conserved but some energy turns into heat, sound and bent metal."],["Newton’s cradle","In a Newton’s cradle, a row of hanging steel balls, lifting and releasing one ball sends one ball flying out the other end, a demonstration of conservation of momentum and energy."],["Spinning","Angular momentum is the momentum of spinning objects. A figure skater spins faster when pulling in their arms because angular momentum is conserved."]],
k:[["Formula","Momentum = mass × velocity"],["Unit","Kilogram meters per second"],["Conservation","Total momentum stays the same in a closed system"],["Change requires","Impulse (force × time)"],["Safety","Airbags and crumple zones spread out impact"],["Spinning version","Angular momentum"]],
d:[["1644","René Descartes describes ‘quantity of motion’"],["1687","Newton’s laws of motion published"],["1967","Newton’s cradle becomes a popular desk toy"]],
s2:[["Rocket in space","Rockets do not need air to push against. They move by throwing mass backward, which is why they work in space."],["Sports","Hitting a ball with a bat or racket transfers momentum. Following through keeps contact longer, sending the ball faster."]],
g:[["Momentum","Mass multiplied by velocity"],["Velocity","Speed in a particular direction"],["Impulse","Force multiplied by the time it acts"],["Collision","When objects hit each other"]],
f:["Momentum is mass times velocity.","Momentum is conserved in collisions.","Airbags reduce force by spreading out the impact."],
fun:"A figure skater spins faster by pulling in their arms, thanks to angular momentum."});

ADDART({id:"pendulums",t:"Pendulums",c:"Science",i:"🕰️",w:"Pendulum",r:["galileo","newton","clocks","grav","chaostheory"],
p:[["A swinging weight","A weight hanging from a pivot."],["Steady timing","Each swing takes the same time."],["Clocks and Earth’s spin","Pendulums kept time and proved Earth rotates."]],
l:"A pendulum is a weight, called a bob, hanging from a fixed point so that it can swing freely back and forth. When pulled to one side and released, gravity pulls it back down, it overshoots and swings up the other side, and the motion repeats. The time for one complete swing is called the period. Around 1602, the Italian scientist Galileo Galilei discovered that, for small swings, the period of a pendulum hardly depends on how wide it swings or how heavy the bob is, but only on its length: a longer pendulum swings more slowly. A pendulum about 1 meter long takes about 1 second for each swing from one side to the other. This regular timing made pendulums ideal for keeping time, and in 1656 the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, which was far more accurate than earlier clocks. Pendulum clocks were the most accurate timekeepers for nearly 300 years. In 1851 the French physicist Léon Foucault used a giant pendulum in Paris to show that Earth rotates, and pendulums have also been used to measure gravity and detect earthquakes.",
s:[["Galileo’s observation","Legend says Galileo noticed the regular swing of a lamp in Pisa Cathedral and timed it against his pulse. He later studied pendulums carefully and suggested using them in clocks."],["Pendulum clocks","Huygens’s clock reduced timekeeping errors from about 15 minutes a day to about 15 seconds. Grandfather clocks use a pendulum about 1 meter long that swings once each second. Big Ben in London has a pendulum about 4 meters long."],["Foucault’s pendulum","Foucault hung a 28-kilogram bob on a 67-meter wire in the Panthéon in Paris. As the hours passed, the direction of its swing slowly turned, because Earth was rotating beneath it. Copies hang in science museums around the world."],["Chaotic pendulums","A double pendulum, one pendulum hanging from another, moves in wild, unpredictable ways, a classic example of chaos."]],
k:[["Parts","Bob, string or rod, and pivot"],["Period depends on","Length (and gravity)"],["1-meter pendulum","About 1 second per swing"],["Pendulum clock invented","1656, Christiaan Huygens"],["Proved Earth rotates","Foucault, 1851"],["Studied by","Galileo (about 1602)"]],
d:[["c. 1602","Galileo studies pendulums"],["1656","Huygens builds the first pendulum clock"],["1851","Foucault’s pendulum in Paris"],["1920s","Quartz clocks begin to replace pendulums for precise time"]],
s2:[["Measuring gravity","Because a pendulum’s period depends on gravity, scientists used pendulums to measure tiny differences in gravity around the world."],["Swings","A playground swing is a pendulum. Pumping your legs at the right moment adds energy and makes you swing higher."]],
g:[["Pendulum","A weight swinging from a fixed point"],["Bob","The weight at the end of a pendulum"],["Period","The time for one complete swing"],["Pivot","The fixed point something turns around"]],
f:["A pendulum’s swing time depends on its length.","Huygens built the first pendulum clock.","Foucault’s pendulum showed that Earth rotates."],
fun:"Big Ben’s clock is fine-tuned by adding or removing old pennies on its pendulum."});

ADDART({id:"artnouveau",t:"Art Nouveau",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🌷",w:"Art Nouveau",r:["artdeco","architecture","graphicdesign","stainedglass","impress"],
p:[["Flowing lines","A style inspired by plants and natural curves."],["Around 1890–1914","It swept across Europe and beyond."],["Everywhere","Buildings, posters, jewelry, furniture and metro stations."]],
l:"Art Nouveau, French for ‘new art’, was an international style of art, architecture and design that flourished from about 1890 to 1914. Rejecting the copying of older historical styles, its artists took inspiration from nature, using long, flowing, curving lines like plant stems, vines, flowers, insects and women’s flowing hair. Art Nouveau aimed to unite fine art with everyday objects, so it appeared not only in paintings and buildings but also in furniture, jewelry, glassware, posters, book designs, wallpaper and even metro station entrances. It was called by different names in different countries: Jugendstil in Germany, Secession style in Austria, Modernisme in Catalonia and Stile Liberty in Italy. Famous works include Alphonse Mucha’s posters in Paris, Hector Guimard’s cast-iron entrances to the Paris Métro, Victor Horta’s houses in Brussels, the glass of Louis Comfort Tiffany and Émile Gallé, Gustav Klimt’s golden paintings in Vienna, and Antoni Gaudí’s buildings in Barcelona. The style faded after the First World War, giving way to the sleeker geometry of Art Deco.",
s:[["Posters","Alphonse Mucha, a Czech artist working in Paris, became famous in 1895 for his poster of the actress Sarah Bernhardt. His posters, with elegant women framed by flowers and halos, defined the style for many people."],["Architecture","Victor Horta’s Hôtel Tassel in Brussels (1893) is often called the first Art Nouveau building, with iron columns shaped like plant stems. Horta’s houses are a UNESCO World Heritage Site, as are Gaudí’s works in Barcelona."],["Glass and jewelry","Louis Comfort Tiffany made colorful leaded-glass lamps and windows in New York, while René Lalique created jewelry and glass with dragonflies, flowers and female figures."],["Around the world","Art Nouveau buildings can be found from Riga in Latvia, which has hundreds, to Budapest, Prague, Glasgow, where Charles Rennie Mackintosh developed his own style, Buenos Aires and Moscow."]],
k:[["Period","About 1890–1914"],["Meaning","New art"],["Inspiration","Plants, flowers and natural curves"],["Other names","Jugendstil, Secession, Modernisme, Liberty"],["Famous artists","Mucha, Horta, Guimard, Tiffany, Klimt, Gaudí"],["Followed by","Art Deco"]],
d:[["1893","Horta’s Hôtel Tassel built in Brussels"],["1895","Mucha’s Gismonda poster"],["1897","Vienna Secession founded"],["1900","Paris Métro entrances and the Paris World’s Fair"]],
s2:[["The name","The style was named after a Paris shop, La Maison de l’Art Nouveau, opened by the art dealer Siegfried Bing in 1895."],["Whiplash","A long, S-shaped curve typical of the style is called the ‘whiplash’ line."]],
g:[["Art Nouveau","A decorative style of flowing natural curves"],["Jugendstil","The German name for Art Nouveau"],["Ornament","Decoration added to an object or building"],["Poster","A large printed picture used to advertise"]],
f:["Art Nouveau was inspired by nature.","Alphonse Mucha made Art Nouveau posters.","Art Nouveau was followed by Art Deco."],
fun:"Some Paris Métro entrances still have their original green Art Nouveau ironwork shaped like plant stems."});

ADDART({id:"artdeco",t:"Art Deco",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🏙️",w:"Art Deco",r:["artnouveau","architecture","skyscrapers","cubism","jazz","bauhaus"],
p:[["Bold and glamorous","Geometric shapes, zigzags and sunbursts."],["The Roaring Twenties","It peaked in the 1920s and 1930s."],["Skyscrapers","The Chrysler Building is a famous example."]],
l:"Art Deco was a style of architecture, design and visual art that flourished from the 1920s to about 1940, especially between the two world wars. It took its name from the 1925 International Exhibition of Modern Decorative and Industrial Arts in Paris. Where Art Nouveau used soft, flowing natural curves, Art Deco favored bold geometric shapes: zigzags, chevrons, stepped forms, sunbursts and streamlined curves, often in luxurious materials such as chrome, lacquer, glass, marble and exotic woods. It drew on many sources, including cubism, the machine age, ancient Egyptian art after the discovery of Tutankhamun’s tomb in 1922, and Aztec and Mayan designs. Art Deco celebrated modern life, speed and technology, and appeared in skyscrapers, cinemas, ocean liners, trains, cars, radios, furniture, jewelry, fashion and posters. Famous examples include the Chrysler Building and Empire State Building in New York, the Christ the Redeemer statue in Rio de Janeiro, and whole districts in Miami Beach, Mumbai and Napier in New Zealand, which was rebuilt in the style after a 1931 earthquake.",
s:[["Skyscrapers","The Chrysler Building (1930), with its gleaming stainless-steel crown of sunburst arches, and the Empire State Building (1931) are among the most famous Art Deco buildings. Their stepped shapes were partly due to New York laws requiring buildings to let light reach the streets."],["Cities of Art Deco","Mumbai has one of the world’s largest collections of Art Deco buildings, part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Miami Beach has hundreds of pastel-colored Art Deco hotels, and Napier, New Zealand, rebuilt its center in the style."],["Streamlining","In the 1930s, Streamline Moderne, a later form of Art Deco, used smooth curves and horizontal lines inspired by fast trains, ships and airplanes, appearing even on toasters and radios."],["Fashion and posters","Art Deco posters by artists such as Cassandre advertised ocean liners and trains with bold shapes and lettering. The flapper dresses and geometric jewelry of the 1920s also belong to the style."]],
k:[["Period","About 1920s–1940"],["Named after","1925 Paris exhibition"],["Features","Geometric shapes, zigzags, sunbursts, streamlining"],["Materials","Chrome, glass, lacquer, marble"],["Famous buildings","Chrysler Building, Empire State Building"],["Art Deco cities","Mumbai, Miami Beach, Napier"]],
d:[["1922","Tutankhamun’s tomb discovered, inspiring Egyptian motifs"],["1925","Paris exhibition gives the style its name"],["1930","Chrysler Building completed"],["1931","Christ the Redeemer statue completed; Napier earthquake"]],
s2:[["The name","The term Art Deco only became common in the 1960s, when the style was rediscovered."],["Ocean liners","The French liner Normandie, launched in 1932, was famous for its luxurious Art Deco interiors."]],
g:[["Art Deco","A bold geometric design style of the 1920s and 1930s"],["Chevron","A V-shaped pattern"],["Streamline","Smooth shape that reduces air resistance"],["Motif","A repeated design or pattern"]],
f:["Art Deco used bold geometric shapes.","The Chrysler Building is Art Deco.","Art Deco was named after a 1925 Paris exhibition."],
fun:"Napier in New Zealand was rebuilt almost entirely in Art Deco style after an earthquake in 1931."});

ADDART({id:"abstractexpressionism",t:"Abstract Expressionism",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎨",w:"Abstract expressionism",r:["modernart","renais","surrealism","popart","cubism","museums"],
p:[["Painting feelings","Artists expressed emotion through color, gesture and scale."],["New York in the 1940s","It made New York a center of the art world."],["Drips and color fields","Jackson Pollock and Mark Rothko are famous examples."]],
l:"Abstract Expressionism was an art movement that developed in New York in the 1940s and 1950s. Its painters created abstract works, without recognizable objects, that aimed to express emotions, energy and the inner self through color, shape, gesture and the act of painting itself. Many worked on enormous canvases that surround the viewer. The movement had two main approaches. ‘Action painters’, such as Jackson Pollock, Willem de Kooning and Franz Kline, used energetic, sweeping brushstrokes; Pollock famously laid his canvases on the floor and dripped, poured and flicked paint onto them. ‘Color field’ painters, such as Mark Rothko, Barnett Newman and Helen Frankenthaler, covered large areas with flat or softly glowing color, aiming to create calm, meditative or overwhelming experiences. Influenced by surrealism’s interest in the unconscious and by European artists who had fled to New York during the Second World War, Abstract Expressionism was the first American art movement to gain worldwide influence, shifting the center of the art world from Paris to New York. Women artists, including Lee Krasner, Joan Mitchell and Elaine de Kooning, were important members.",
s:[["Jackson Pollock","Pollock developed his ‘drip’ technique around 1947, moving around canvases on the floor of his barn studio and letting paint fall in looping lines. Works such as Autumn Rhythm (1950) are full of layered tangles of paint. Photographs and films of him at work made the act of painting famous."],["Mark Rothko","Rothko’s mature paintings consist of large, soft-edged rectangles of color floating on a colored background. He hoped viewers would have deep emotional responses. The Rothko Chapel in Houston, opened in 1971, holds 14 of his dark paintings."],["Women of the movement","Lee Krasner, Pollock’s wife, was a major painter in her own right. Joan Mitchell made lively, colorful paintings inspired by landscapes, and Helen Frankenthaler invented the ‘soak-stain’ technique of pouring thinned paint onto raw canvas."],["Debates","Critics such as Clement Greenberg championed the movement; others found it baffling. Historians have also noted that the US government promoted it abroad during the Cold War as a symbol of artistic freedom."]],
k:[["Began","1940s, New York"],["Main approaches","Action painting and color field painting"],["Famous artists","Pollock, de Kooning, Rothko, Krasner, Mitchell, Frankenthaler"],["Key idea","Expressing emotion through abstract painting"],["Typical scale","Very large canvases"],["Influence","First American art movement with global impact"]],
d:[["1943","Pollock’s first solo exhibition"],["1947","Pollock begins drip painting"],["1952","Frankenthaler paints Mountains and Sea"],["1971","Rothko Chapel opens in Houston"]],
s2:[["Record prices","Paintings by Pollock, de Kooning and Rothko have sold for well over 100 million US dollars."],["Drips and physics","Some scientists have studied Pollock’s drip patterns and found fractal-like structures, though this remains debated."]],
g:[["Abstract art","Art that does not show recognizable objects"],["Action painting","Painting with energetic gestures"],["Color field","Painting with large areas of flat color"],["Canvas","A cloth surface for painting"]],
f:["Abstract Expressionism began in New York.","Jackson Pollock dripped paint onto canvases.","Mark Rothko painted large fields of color."],
fun:"Jackson Pollock sometimes used sticks, knives and even turkey basters instead of brushes."});

ADDART({id:"bauhaus",t:"The Bauhaus",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📐",w:"Bauhaus",r:["architecture","artdeco","modernart","germanycountry","skyscrapers","typography"],
p:[["A famous art school","Founded in Germany in 1919."],["Form follows function","Simple, useful, beautiful design."],["Still everywhere","Its ideas shaped modern furniture, buildings and graphics."]],
l:"The Bauhaus was a German school of art, architecture and design that operated from 1919 to 1933 and became one of the most influential forces in modern design. Founded by the architect Walter Gropius in Weimar, it aimed to bring together art, craft and industry, training students to design beautiful, practical objects and buildings that could be mass-produced for everyday people. Its teachers included some of the leading artists of the time, such as Wassily Kandinsky, Paul Klee, László Moholy-Nagy and Josef Albers. Bauhaus design favored simple geometric shapes, primary colors, clean lines, new materials such as steel tubing and glass, and the idea that an object’s form should follow its function, without unnecessary decoration. In 1925 the school moved to Dessau, into a striking glass-walled building designed by Gropius, and in 1932 to Berlin. The Nazi government, which considered its art ‘degenerate’, forced it to close in 1933. Many teachers and students emigrated to the United States, Britain, Israel and elsewhere, spreading Bauhaus ideas around the world. Tel Aviv’s ‘White City’ contains thousands of Bauhaus-style buildings.",
s:[["Teaching","Every student began with a preliminary course exploring materials, color and form, then trained in workshops such as metalwork, weaving, furniture, pottery and typography. Students learned to design for industrial production."],["Famous designs","Marcel Breuer’s tubular steel chairs, Marianne Brandt’s metal lamps and teapots, Anni Albers’s woven textiles and Herbert Bayer’s simple lowercase typefaces all came from the Bauhaus."],["Mies van der Rohe","The architect Ludwig Mies van der Rohe, the last director of the Bauhaus, later designed glass-and-steel skyscrapers in the United States and is famous for the phrase ‘less is more’."],["World Heritage","The Bauhaus buildings in Weimar and Dessau, and the ‘White City’ of Tel Aviv, are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."]],
k:[["Years","1919–1933"],["Founder","Walter Gropius"],["Locations","Weimar, Dessau, Berlin"],["Famous teachers","Kandinsky, Klee, Moholy-Nagy, Albers, Mies van der Rohe"],["Principle","Form follows function"],["Closed by","Nazi pressure, 1933"]],
d:[["1919","Bauhaus founded in Weimar"],["1925","Moves to Dessau"],["1926","Dessau Bauhaus building opens"],["1932","Moves to Berlin"],["1933","School closes"],["2019","Centenary celebrated worldwide"]],
s2:[["The name","Bauhaus means ‘building house’ in German."],["Women at the Bauhaus","Many women studied at the Bauhaus, but they were often directed toward weaving. Some, like Anni Albers and Gunta Stölzl, became leading designers."]],
g:[["Bauhaus","A German school of modern design"],["Functionalism","Designing according to purpose"],["Typography","The design of letters and text"],["Mass production","Making many identical products in factories"]],
f:["The Bauhaus was founded by Walter Gropius.","Bauhaus design favored simple shapes.","The Bauhaus closed in 1933."],
fun:"Tel Aviv has more Bauhaus-style buildings than any other city in the world."});

ADDART({id:"expressionism",t:"Expressionism",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"😱",w:"Expressionism",r:["modernart","renais","impress","cinema","abstractexpressionism","cubism"],
p:[["Feelings over appearance","Artists distorted reality to express emotion."],["The Scream","Edvard Munch’s painting is its best-known image."],["Art, film and theater","It spread from painting to cinema and stage."]],
l:"Expressionism was a movement in art, literature, theater, music and film that flourished in the early 20th century, especially in Germany and Austria from about 1905 to the 1920s. Instead of showing the world as it looks, Expressionist artists distorted shapes, exaggerated colors and used bold, rough brushstrokes to express strong emotions such as fear, anxiety, longing and excitement. They often reacted against the pressures of modern city life and, later, the horrors of the First World War. An important forerunner was the Norwegian painter Edvard Munch, whose painting The Scream (1893), showing a figure clutching its face under a blood-red sky, became a symbol of modern anxiety. In Germany, two groups led the movement: Die Brücke (The Bridge), founded in Dresden in 1905 by Ernst Ludwig Kirchner and others, and Der Blaue Reiter (The Blue Rider), founded in Munich in 1911 by Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc. Expressionism also shaped film, as in the eerie, shadowy sets of The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari (1920) and Metropolis (1927), and influenced horror movies and film noir.",
s:[["The Scream","Munch made several versions of The Scream in paint, pastel and print. He described feeling ‘an infinite scream passing through nature’ while walking at sunset. One version sold at auction in 2012 for about 120 million US dollars."],["Die Brücke and Der Blaue Reiter","Die Brücke artists painted city streets and nudes in harsh colors and jagged shapes. Der Blaue Reiter artists were more spiritual; Franz Marc painted blue horses and red deer, while Kandinsky moved toward pure abstraction."],["Expressionist film","German Expressionist films used painted, crooked sets, dramatic lighting and deep shadows. Nosferatu (1922), an early vampire film, and Metropolis, a science-fiction epic, influenced filmmakers for generations."],["Suppressed","In 1937 the Nazi government seized thousands of Expressionist works and mocked them in a ‘Degenerate Art’ exhibition. Many artists were banned from working or fled abroad."]],
k:[["Period","About 1905–1920s"],["Centered in","Germany and Austria"],["Key idea","Expressing emotion through distortion"],["Forerunner","Edvard Munch, The Scream (1893)"],["Groups","Die Brücke (1905), Der Blaue Reiter (1911)"],["In film","The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari, Metropolis"]],
d:[["1893","Munch paints The Scream"],["1905","Die Brücke founded in Dresden"],["1911","Der Blaue Reiter founded in Munich"],["1920","The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari released"],["1937","Nazi ‘Degenerate Art’ exhibition"]],
s2:[["Van Gogh’s influence","The swirling brushwork and intense colors of Vincent van Gogh strongly influenced the Expressionists."],["Music","The composer Arnold Schoenberg, who also painted, wrote unsettling Expressionist music that broke traditional rules of harmony."]],
g:[["Expressionism","Art that expresses emotion through distortion"],["Distortion","Changing a shape from its normal form"],["Forerunner","Someone who comes before and influences others"],["Film noir","A dark, shadowy style of crime film"]],
f:["Edvard Munch painted The Scream.","Expressionists distorted shapes to show emotion.","Expressionism influenced cinema."],
fun:"The Scream has been stolen twice, in 1994 and 2004, and recovered both times."});

ADDART({id:"tapestry",t:"Tapestry and Textile Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🧶",w:"Tapestry",r:["weaving","silk","cotton","medieurope","museums","clothing","flaxlinen"],
p:[["Pictures woven in thread","Tapestries are images woven on a loom."],["The Bayeux Tapestry","A famous 70-meter embroidery of 1066."],["Textile traditions","Every culture has its own woven art."]],
l:"A tapestry is a heavy textile in which a picture or design is woven directly into the fabric on a loom, using colored threads, usually wool, sometimes with silk, gold or silver. Unlike ordinary weaving, where colored threads run all the way across, tapestry weavers pass each colored thread back and forth only in the area where that color is needed, building up the image bit by bit. Tapestries have been made for thousands of years, in ancient Egypt, China, Peru and elsewhere. In medieval and Renaissance Europe, huge tapestries hung on castle and palace walls, keeping rooms warm and displaying the wealth of their owners; they could be rolled up and carried from place to place. Centers such as Arras, Brussels and later the Gobelins workshop in Paris became famous for them. The Bayeux Tapestry, showing the Norman conquest of England in 1066, is actually an embroidery, with stitches sewn onto linen, but it is the world’s best-known ‘tapestry’. Textile art more broadly includes quilting, embroidery, batik, kente cloth, ikat, rugs and carpets, and contemporary artists continue to use textiles as a medium.",
s:[["The Bayeux Tapestry","About 70 meters long and 50 centimeters high, it tells the story of William the Conqueror’s invasion of England and the Battle of Hastings in 58 scenes, probably made in England in the 1070s. It is normally kept in Bayeux, France, but went on show at the British Museum in London in September 2026 on a loan lasting until 2027."],["The Unicorn Tapestries","Two famous sets of tapestries from around 1500, The Lady and the Unicorn in Paris and The Hunt of the Unicorn in New York, show detailed scenes against backgrounds full of flowers, a style called millefleur, meaning ‘a thousand flowers’."],["Textiles around the world","Kente cloth from Ghana is woven in narrow strips of bright, symbolic patterns. Ikat, made in Indonesia, Central Asia and Latin America, uses threads dyed before weaving. Andean peoples have woven fine textiles for thousands of years, and Persian and Turkish carpets are famous worldwide."],["Modern textile art","20th-century artists such as Anni Albers and Sheila Hicks, and contemporary artists such as El Anatsui, who makes shimmering hangings from bottle caps, have made textiles a major art form."]],
k:[["Tapestry","A picture woven on a loom"],["Common material","Wool, sometimes silk and metal threads"],["Famous European centers","Arras, Brussels, Gobelins (Paris)"],["Bayeux Tapestry","About 70 m long; really an embroidery"],["Other textile arts","Quilting, embroidery, batik, kente, ikat, carpets"]],
d:[["c. 1070s","Bayeux Tapestry made"],["c. 1500","Unicorn tapestries woven"],["1662","Gobelins workshop becomes the French royal factory"],["2007","Bayeux Tapestry added to UNESCO’s Memory of the World"]],
s2:[["Slow work","A skilled weaver might complete only about a square meter of fine tapestry in a month."],["Bayeux in Britain","The Bayeux Tapestry’s loan to the British Museum, which opened in September 2026, is its first display in England in more than 900 years."]],
g:[["Tapestry","A woven picture"],["Loom","A frame or machine for weaving"],["Embroidery","Designs sewn onto fabric"],["Warp and weft","Lengthwise and crosswise threads in weaving"]],
f:["Tapestries are woven on a loom.","The Bayeux Tapestry is really an embroidery.","Kente is a woven cloth from Ghana."],
fun:"The Bayeux Tapestry is about as long as six school buses parked end to end."});

ADDART({id:"saffron",t:"Saffron",c:"Plants",i:"🌸",w:"Saffron",r:["spice","flower","herbs","irancountry","silk","vanilla"],
p:[["The costliest spice","Saffron can cost more by weight than silver."],["From a crocus","It is the dried threads of a purple flower."],["Picked by hand","Each flower has only three threads."]],
l:"Saffron is a spice made from the dried stigmas, the thread-like parts of the female flower organ, of the saffron crocus (Crocus sativus), a purple flower that blooms in autumn. Each flower has only three red stigmas, which must be picked by hand, usually early in the morning, and then dried. It takes roughly 150,000 flowers to produce 1 kilogram of saffron, which is why it is the most expensive spice in the world by weight. Saffron gives food a golden-yellow color, a distinctive flavor and a honey-like aroma, and is used in dishes such as Spanish paella, Italian risotto alla milanese, Indian biryani and Persian rice, as well as in sweets and drinks. It has also been used as a dye and in traditional medicine. The saffron crocus cannot grow from seed; it is spread by planting underground bulb-like corms. It was probably first cultivated in Greece or nearby regions thousands of years ago and later spread to Persia, India, Spain and elsewhere. Today Iran produces around 90 percent of the world’s saffron, with smaller amounts from Afghanistan, India (Kashmir), Spain, Greece and Morocco.",
s:[["Harvest","The flowers bloom for only a few weeks in October and November, and each lasts just a day or two. Families and workers pick them quickly, then separate the stigmas by hand, often sitting around tables for hours."],["Ancient history","A wall painting from about 3,600 years ago on the Greek island of Santorini shows women gathering saffron. Saffron was traded along ancient routes and used by Egyptians, Greeks and Romans."],["Fake saffron","Because it is so valuable, saffron is sometimes faked with safflower, marigold petals, dyed corn silk or other materials. Real saffron threads release their color slowly in water."],["Color","Saffron’s color comes from a pigment called crocin. Saffron-colored robes are worn by Buddhist monks in some traditions, though today they are usually dyed with cheaper materials."]],
k:[["Comes from","Stigmas of the saffron crocus"],["Threads per flower","Three"],["Flowers per kilogram","About 150,000"],["Biggest producer","Iran (about 90%)"],["Color pigment","Crocin"],["Famous dishes","Paella, risotto, biryani, Persian rice"]],
s2:[["A little goes far","Only a pinch of saffron, a few threads, is needed to color and flavor a whole dish."],["A sterile flower","The saffron crocus is a sterile plant that cannot produce seeds, so every saffron crocus in the world was grown from a corm."]],
g:[["Stigma","The part of a flower that receives pollen"],["Corm","A swollen underground stem that stores food"],["Spice","A dried plant part used to flavor food"],["Crocin","The pigment that gives saffron its color"]],
f:["Saffron comes from a crocus flower.","Saffron is picked by hand.","Iran produces most of the world’s saffron."],
fun:"It takes about 150,000 crocus flowers to make one kilogram of saffron."});

ADDART({id:"lavender",t:"Lavender",c:"Plants",i:"💜",w:"Lavandula",r:["herbs","flower","bee","med","medicinalplants"],
p:[["Fragrant purple flowers","Lavender is famous for its scent."],["Mediterranean origins","It grows wild in sunny, dry hills."],["Many uses","Perfume, soap, cooking and gardens."]],
l:"Lavender is a group of about 45 species of flowering plants in the mint family, native to the Mediterranean region, North Africa, the Middle East and India. They are small, woody shrubs with narrow, gray-green leaves and spikes of tiny purple, blue, pink or white flowers. Both the flowers and leaves contain fragrant oils, which give lavender its famous sweet, herbal scent. Lavender grows best in sunny places with poor, well-drained soil, and needs little water once established, so it thrives in dry climates. The plant has been used for thousands of years: the ancient Romans added it to baths, and its name may come from the Latin ‘lavare’, meaning ‘to wash’. Today lavender oil, extracted by steam distillation, is widely used in perfumes, soaps, cosmetics and cleaning products, and dried lavender is used in sachets to scent clothes. Some types are used in cooking, in herb mixes, baking and drinks. Lavender fields, such as those in Provence in southern France, Hokkaido in Japan and the Cotswolds in England, attract many visitors when they bloom in summer. Its flowers are also rich in nectar and loved by bees.",
s:[["Types","English lavender (Lavandula angustifolia) is prized for its sweet oil and hardiness. French or Spanish lavender has distinctive ‘rabbit ear’ petals at the top of each flower head. Lavandin, a hybrid, produces large amounts of oil and is widely grown commercially."],["Making oil","Freshly cut lavender is packed into a still, and steam is passed through it to carry the fragrant oils away. When the steam cools, the oil separates from the water and is collected."],["Provence","The lavender fields of the Valensole plateau in Provence bloom in purple stripes in late June and July. France is one of the world’s leading producers of lavender oil, along with Bulgaria."],["Wildlife","Bees, butterflies and other pollinators visit lavender flowers. Gardeners plant it to attract pollinators and because deer and rabbits usually avoid its strong scent."]],
k:[["Family","Mint family (Lamiaceae)"],["Species","About 45"],["Native to","Mediterranean, North Africa, Middle East, India"],["Grows best in","Sun and dry, poor soil"],["Oil made by","Steam distillation"],["Famous fields","Provence, Hokkaido, the Cotswolds"]],
s2:[["Calming scent","Many people find lavender’s scent relaxing, and it is widely used in products meant to aid sleep, though scientific evidence for its effects is mixed."],["Not for every garden","Lavender usually dies in wet, heavy soil, because its roots rot without good drainage."]],
g:[["Shrub","A small, woody plant with many stems"],["Essential oil","A concentrated scented oil from a plant"],["Distillation","Separating substances by heating and cooling"],["Hybrid","A cross between two species or varieties"]],
f:["Lavender is in the mint family.","Lavender oil is made by steam distillation.","Bees love lavender flowers."],
fun:"Lavender’s name may come from the Latin word for ‘wash’, because Romans scented their baths with it."});

ADDART({id:"agave",t:"Agaves",c:"Plants",i:"🌵",w:"Agave",r:["cactus","desert","mexicocountry","bat","cotton"],
p:[["Desert succulents","Spiky rosettes that store water in thick leaves."],["Flowering once","Many bloom once after decades, then die."],["Tequila and fiber","Agaves give drinks, syrup and rope."]],
l:"Agaves are a group of about 200 to 270 species of succulent plants native to the hot, dry regions of the Americas, especially Mexico, as well as the southwestern United States, Central America and the Caribbean. They grow as large rosettes of thick, fleshy, often spiny leaves that store water, helping them survive long droughts. Most agaves live for many years, 10 to 30 or more, before flowering just once: they send up a tall flower stalk, sometimes over 8 meters high, that grows astonishingly fast, then the main plant dies, leaving behind offshoots and seeds. One species, Agave americana, is called the century plant because people once thought it took 100 years to bloom. Agaves have been important to people for thousands of years. Their leaves give strong fibers, such as sisal and henequen, used for rope, mats and twine. The sweet sap is made into agave syrup and traditional drinks such as pulque, and the cooked hearts of blue agave and other species are fermented and distilled to make tequila and mezcal. Their night-blooming flowers are pollinated by bats.",
s:[["Tequila and mezcal","Tequila is made only from blue agave (Agave tequilana) grown in certain regions of Mexico, especially Jalisco. Mezcal can be made from many agave species. Workers called jimadores harvest the agave hearts, called piñas, which can weigh over 50 kilograms."],["Bat partners","Many agaves bloom at night, producing nectar for long-nosed bats, which carry pollen from plant to plant. Some tequila producers let part of their crop flower to help bats, labeled ‘bat-friendly’."],["Fiber","Sisal, from Agave sisalana, is grown in Brazil, Tanzania, Kenya and elsewhere and made into rope, twine, dartboards and carpets. Henequen was once a major crop in Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula."],["Not a cactus","Agaves are often mistaken for cacti, but they are more closely related to asparagus and are part of the same family as yucca."]],
k:[["Species","About 200–270"],["Native to","The Americas, especially Mexico"],["Type","Succulent rosette"],["Flowering","Usually once, after many years"],["Pollinators","Bats"],["Products","Tequila, mezcal, syrup, sisal fiber"]],
d:[["c. 9,000 years ago","People in Mexico cooking agave"],["1974","Tequila receives protected origin status in Mexico"],["2006","Agave landscape of Tequila named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Fast growth","An agave flower stalk can grow more than 10 centimeters in a single day."],["Living fences","In parts of Mexico and the Mediterranean, people plant agaves in rows as spiny living fences."]],
g:[["Succulent","A plant that stores water in thick leaves or stems"],["Rosette","A circle of leaves growing from one point"],["Monocarpic","Flowering only once before dying"],["Sisal","A strong fiber from agave leaves"]],
f:["Agaves store water in their leaves.","Many agaves flower only once.","Tequila is made from blue agave."],
fun:"The century plant does not really take 100 years to bloom; it usually takes 10 to 30."});

ADDART({id:"versioncontrol",t:"Version Control and Git",c:"Technology",i:"🌿",w:"Version control",r:["coding","linux","opensource","cloud","algorithm","comp","softwarebugs"],
p:[["Saving every version","Tracking changes to files over time."],["Teamwork for coders","Many people can work on the same code."],["Git","The most widely used system, created in 2005."]],
l:"Version control is a system for recording changes to files over time, so that you can see who changed what and when, compare versions, and go back to earlier ones if something goes wrong. It is used mainly by software developers to manage code, but also for documents, websites, data and design files. Version control lets many people work on the same project at once: each person can make changes in their own copy, called a branch, and then merge their work back together, with the system helping to spot conflicts where two people changed the same lines. The most widely used version control system today is Git, created in 2005 by Linus Torvalds, the creator of the Linux operating system, to manage Linux’s development. Git is distributed, meaning every developer has a full copy of the project’s history on their own computer. Online services such as GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket host Git projects, letting people share code, review each other’s changes and collaborate on open-source software with contributors around the world.",
s:[["Key ideas","A repository, or repo, holds a project and its history. A commit is a saved snapshot of changes with a message describing them. A branch is a separate line of work. A merge combines branches. A pull request, or merge request, asks others to review and accept changes."],["Before Git","Early systems included SCCS (1972), RCS (1982), CVS (1990) and Subversion (2000), which mostly stored history on a central server. Linux used a commercial system called BitKeeper until 2005, when a dispute led Torvalds to write Git in about two weeks."],["GitHub","GitHub, launched in 2008 and bought by Microsoft in 2018, became the largest host of source code in the world, with many millions of developers and repositories, including much of the world’s open-source software."],["Beyond code","Writers, scientists and governments also use version control. Some countries publish their laws in Git repositories so anyone can see how they change."]],
k:[["Purpose","Tracking changes to files"],["Most popular system","Git (2005)"],["Created by","Linus Torvalds"],["Key terms","Repository, commit, branch, merge"],["Hosting services","GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket"],["Type","Distributed version control"]],
d:[["1972","SCCS, an early version control system"],["2000","Subversion released"],["2005","Git created"],["2008","GitHub launches"],["2018","Microsoft buys GitHub"]],
s2:[["The name","Torvalds joked that he named Git after himself; ‘git’ is British slang for an unpleasant person."],["Arctic vault","In 2020 GitHub stored a copy of many public open-source projects on film in the Arctic World Archive in Svalbard, Norway, designed to last 1,000 years."]],
g:[["Repository","A project and its stored history"],["Commit","A saved snapshot of changes"],["Branch","A separate line of development"],["Merge","Combining changes from different branches"]],
f:["Git was created by Linus Torvalds.","Version control tracks changes to files.","Branches let people work separately and then merge."],
fun:"Linus Torvalds wrote the first version of Git in about two weeks."});

ADDART({id:"logicgates",t:"Logic Gates and Boolean Logic",c:"Technology",i:"🔌",w:"Logic gate",r:["binary","comp","logic","turing","algorithm"],
p:[["True or false","Boolean logic works with just two values."],["AND, OR, NOT","Simple rules combine signals."],["Inside every chip","Billions of logic gates make computers work."]],
l:"Boolean logic is a kind of algebra that works with only two values, true and false, often written as 1 and 0. It was developed by the English mathematician George Boole, who published it in 1847 and 1854. Boolean logic uses a few basic operations. AND gives true only if both inputs are true; OR gives true if at least one input is true; NOT flips true to false and false to true. Others include NAND (not and), NOR (not or) and XOR (exclusive or, true if exactly one input is true). In 1937 the American engineer Claude Shannon showed that electrical switching circuits could carry out Boolean logic, laying the foundation for digital computers. A logic gate is a tiny electronic circuit that performs one of these operations on electrical signals, where high voltage stands for 1 and low voltage for 0. Logic gates are built from transistors, and by combining many gates, engineers create circuits that add numbers, store data and make decisions. A modern computer processor contains billions of transistors forming vast numbers of logic gates, switching billions of times every second.",
s:[["Truth tables","A truth table lists every possible combination of inputs and the resulting output. For example, the AND gate gives 1 only for inputs 1 and 1, while the OR gate gives 0 only for inputs 0 and 0."],["Building a calculator","Combining an XOR gate and an AND gate makes a half adder, which adds two binary digits. Chaining adders together lets a circuit add large numbers, the basis of a computer’s arithmetic."],["Universal gates","NAND and NOR gates are called universal, because any other logic gate, and therefore any digital circuit, can be built using only one of them."],["Everyday logic","Search engines use Boolean operators: searching for ‘cats AND dogs’ finds pages with both words, while ‘cats OR dogs’ finds pages with either. Spreadsheets and programming languages use the same ideas."]],
k:[["Values","True and false (1 and 0)"],["Developed by","George Boole (1847, 1854)"],["Basic operations","AND, OR, NOT"],["Linked to circuits","Claude Shannon (1937)"],["Built from","Transistors"],["Universal gates","NAND and NOR"]],
d:[["1847","Boole’s The Mathematical Analysis of Logic"],["1854","Boole’s The Laws of Thought"],["1937","Shannon links Boolean logic to circuits"],["1947","Transistor invented"],["1971","First commercial microprocessor"]],
s2:[["Not just electronics","Logic gates have been built from relays, vacuum tubes, water channels, dominoes and even crabs in a scientific experiment."],["Minecraft computers","Players have built working computers in games such as Minecraft by creating logic gates out of in-game materials."]],
g:[["Boolean logic","Logic using only true and false"],["Logic gate","A circuit performing a logical operation"],["Truth table","A table of all inputs and outputs"],["Transistor","A tiny electronic switch"]],
f:["Boolean logic uses true and false.","Logic gates are built from transistors.","NAND gates can build any circuit."],
fun:"Scientists once built logic gates using swarms of soldier crabs."});

ADDART({id:"sorting",t:"Sorting and Searching",c:"Technology",i:"🔢",w:"Sorting algorithm",r:["algorithm","coding","searchengines","comp","graphtheory","binary","databases"],
p:[["Putting things in order","Sorting arranges data from smallest to largest or A to Z."],["Finding things fast","Searching sorted data can be very quick."],["Clever recipes","Different algorithms suit different jobs."]],
l:"Sorting and searching are two of the most common tasks computers perform. Sorting means arranging items in order, such as numbers from smallest to largest or names alphabetically. Searching means finding a particular item in a collection. Computer scientists have invented many algorithms, step-by-step methods, for each, and compare them by how much time and memory they need as the amount of data grows. Simple sorting methods, such as bubble sort, which repeatedly swaps neighboring items that are out of order, are easy to understand but become very slow with large amounts of data. Faster methods, such as merge sort, invented by John von Neumann in 1945, and quicksort, invented by Tony Hoare in 1959, split the data into smaller parts, sort them and combine the results. For searching, a linear search checks every item one by one, while binary search, which only works on sorted data, repeatedly halves the search range, like looking up a word in a dictionary: it can find one item among a million in about 20 steps. Search engines, databases, spreadsheets and phones all depend on efficient sorting and searching.",
s:[["Bubble sort","In bubble sort, larger items gradually ‘bubble up’ to the end of the list. It is simple but needs roughly n² steps for n items, so sorting 1 million items could take around a trillion steps."],["Divide and conquer","Merge sort splits a list in half, sorts each half and merges them. Quicksort picks a ‘pivot’ item and splits the list into items smaller and larger than it. Both need about n × log n steps, vastly fewer than bubble sort for big lists."],["Binary search","To find a number in a sorted list, binary search checks the middle item, then discards the half that cannot contain the target, and repeats. Each step halves the remaining items, so even huge lists are searched quickly."],["Indexes and hashing","Databases build indexes, like the index at the back of a book, to find records quickly. Hash tables use a formula to jump almost directly to where an item is stored."]],
k:[["Sorting","Arranging items in order"],["Searching","Finding a particular item"],["Slow sort example","Bubble sort"],["Fast sort examples","Merge sort (1945), quicksort (1959)"],["Fast search","Binary search (needs sorted data)"],["One in a million","Binary search needs about 20 steps"]],
d:[["1945","John von Neumann describes merge sort"],["1946","Binary search first described"],["1959","Tony Hoare invents quicksort"],["2002","Timsort created, later used in Python and Java"]],
s2:[["Sorting dances","Videos of folk dancers acting out sorting algorithms, with each dancer as a number, have helped students learn how they work."],["Guess the number","The game ‘I’m thinking of a number between 1 and 100’ can always be won in 7 guesses using binary search."]],
g:[["Algorithm","A step-by-step method for solving a problem"],["Sorting","Putting items in order"],["Binary search","Searching by repeatedly halving"],["Efficiency","How much time or memory an algorithm needs"]],
f:["Binary search only works on sorted data.","Quicksort was invented by Tony Hoare.","Bubble sort is slow for large lists."],
fun:"Using binary search, you can always guess a number between 1 and 100 in seven tries or fewer."});
